  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The proposed pseudo-holdout target is drawn from histories already used to train the frozen model. This supports in-sample sensitivity analysis, but cannot establish held-out predictive validity. Genuine auxiliary holdouts require excluding their targets during diagnostic-model training without changing the host splits.",
    "Equal-size deletions do not isolate recency effects: deleting different positions shifts different surviving positional embeddings. The random control can also delete the same event as the recent-removal intervention. Control eligibility, positional confounds, timestamp treatment, and repeated-item handling need explicit definitions.",
    "Reproduction requires fixed user counts, control repetitions, candidate-sampling rules, tie handling, bootstrap settings, scoring tensor shapes, and missing-category aggregation rules. A four-category macro result should be unavailable if any required category lacks observations.",
    "The diagnostic does not directly measure compatibility, redundancy, or immediate-choice acceptance. Its usefulness for choosing model changes remains untested. Originality 6 and potential 8 are optimistic without comparative evidence; difficulty 3 understates leakage-free validation and architecture-specific integration. Its GPU cost also needs a concrete bound."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Establish how to distinguish descriptive sensitivity on training examples from predictive validity without changing the prescribed benchmark.",
      "query": "Sequential recommendation diagnostic validation: how should chronological auxiliary holdouts be constructed exclusively within training histories when a frozen SASRec model has already trained on the proposed target interactions? Investigate target leakage, cross-fitting or diagnostic-model retraining, and evaluating whether perturbation metrics predict gains on unseen model variants."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Find controls that separate removed information from positional artifacts and make the intervention reproducible.",
      "query": "SASRec history deletion perturbation diagnostics with learned absolute positional embeddings: how can recent nonterminal deletion versus random nonterminal deletion control for position shifts, left padding, truncation, repeated items, and overlapping treatment/control deletions? Compare deletion, masking, position-preserving interventions, and matched controls, including their limitations."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Assess whether this diagnostic deserves priority over simpler baselines and supports the claimed potential.",
      "query": "Evidence that sequential recommendation history-perturbation sensitivity predicts out-of-sample HR@10 improvements across architectures, losses, or negative samplers: compare against popularity, last-item, recency-weighted transition, and training-margin diagnostics. What evaluation controls address adaptive model-selection bias and sampled-candidate versus full-catalog differences?"
    }
  ]
}
